-- title:
--   Lemma 4.3.2 — the inductive subsystem lemma (corrected: $n\ge 2$ and every $d_i\ge 2$)
-- statement:
--   Let $n\ge 2$ and let $\mathcal H=\bigotimes_{i=0}^{n-1}\mathcal H_i$ be a Hilbert space with $n$ subsystems, $\mathcal H_i=\mathbb C^{d_i}$ of dimension
--   $$
--   d_i\ge 2\qquad(i=0,\dots,n-1).
--   $$
--   Let $G$ be any set of unitary operators on $\mathcal H$ (a gate set). Two subsystems $a\neq b$ are *computationally connected* when every operator $V\otimes I$ with $V\in\mathrm{SU}(d_ad_b)$ acting on $\mathcal H_a\otimes\mathcal H_b$ is densely generated by $G$. If the graph on $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ whose edges are the computationally connected pairs is connected, then $G$ densely generates the special unitary group of the whole space:
--   $$
--   \mathrm{SU}\Big(\prod_{i=0}^{n-1} d_i\Big)\subseteq\overline{\langle G\rangle},
--   $$
--   where $\overline{\langle G\rangle}$ is the closure of the set of finite products of gates from $G$.
--
--   The lemma reduces universality of a gate set on many subsystems to universality on the pairs of a connected graph; the book applies it to the controlled-not plus single-qubit gates (§4.3.2).
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: any number n of subsystems → 2 ≤ n; reason: for n = 1 the one-vertex graph is connected with no edge, and G = ∅ with d₁ = 2 does not densely generate SU(2) (it generates only {I})
--   - correction: each subsystem H_i of dimension d_i (d_i ≥ 1) → 2 ≤ d_i for every i; reason: for n = 3, d = (2, 1, 2) and G = {SU(2) ⊗ I on subsystems 1, 2} ∪ {I ⊗ SU(2) on subsystems 2, 3}, both pairs (1,2) and (2,3) are computationally connected, so the graph 1 – 2 – 3 is connected, but G generates the closed group SU(2) ⊗ SU(2), which does not contain SU(4)
--   - reading: subsystem structure H = ⊕_{i=1}^n H_i → the tensor product ⊗_i H_i; reason: the lemma speaks of H_a ⊗ H_b and concludes SU(∏ d_i); a direct sum has dimension Σ d_i and cannot carry SU(∏ d_i)
--   - reading: the gate set can densely generate SU(∏_{i=1}^n d_i) → SU(∏ d_i) ⊆ closure of the finite products of gates; reason: gates may carry global phases, so the densely generated set can be larger than SU; containment is the content of "can densely generate"
--   - generalization: operators on the combined Hilbert space H_a ⊗ H_b densely generate SU(d_a d_b) → every element of SU(d_a d_b) ⊗ I on the pair is densely generated by all of G; reason: the book's condition implies this one, so the book's graph is a subgraph of the Lean graph and connectedness of the book's graph gives the hypothesis
--   - generalization: a gate set (finite, Definition 4.3.1) → any set G of unitaries; reason: finite gate sets are a special case
--   - encoding: subsystems 1, …, n → Fin n (0, …, n−1); the graph via SimpleGraph.fromRel (symmetrized); reason: Lean convention; computational connectedness is symmetric, so same graph
-- source:
--   Bacon, Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, Lemma 4.3.2, p.52 (PDF p.69)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_Universality_denseGen
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_Universality_compGraph

namespace BaconThesis.Universality

/-- **Lemma 4.3.2 (the inductive subsystem lemma), corrected: `n ≥ 2` and every `d_i ≥ 2`.**
Bacon, *Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers*, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1,
§4.3.1, Lemma 4.3.2, p.52 (PDF p.69): "Suppose one is given a Hilbert space H with a subsystem
structure H = ⊕_{i=1}^n Hi, each subsystem Hi of dimension di. We say that two subsystems Ha and Hb
of dimensions da and db are computationally connected if operators on the combined Hilbert space
Ha ⊗ Hb densely generate SU(dadb). Let G be the graph of computationally connected subsystems for a
given gate set. If this graph is connected, then the gate set can densely generate
SU(∏_{i=1}^n di)."

Let `n ≥ 2` subsystems have dimensions `d_0, …, d_{n-1}`, each at least `2`, and let `G` be any set
of unitaries on `H = ⊗_i ℂ^{d_i}`. If the graph of computationally connected subsystems of `G`
(`compGraph d G`) is connected, then every element of `SU(∏_i d_i)` is a limit of finite products
of gates from `G`.

**Formalization Note.** The subsystem structure is read as a tensor product (the printed `⊕` is
inconsistent with `H_a ⊗ H_b` and with `SU(∏ d_i)`). Two hypotheses correct the printed lemma,
which is false without them: `2 ≤ n` (for `n = 1` the one-vertex graph is connected with no edge;
`G = ∅`, `d_0 = 2` generates only `{I}`), and `2 ≤ d i` (for `n = 3`, `d = (2, 1, 2)`, the gates
`SU(2) ⊗ I` on subsystems `0, 1` and `I ⊗ SU(2)` on subsystems `1, 2` make the path `0 – 1 – 2`
connected but generate only the closed group `SU(2) ⊗ SU(2) ⊉ SU(4)`). The conclusion is
containment in the densely generated set; gates may carry global phases, so equality with
`SU(∏ d_i)` is not claimed. `G` may be infinite. Subsystems are indexed from `0`. -/
theorem lemma_4_3_2 {n : ℕ} (hn : 2 ≤ n) (d : Fin n → ℕ) (hd : ∀ i, 2 ≤ d i)
    (G : Set (Matrix.unitaryGroup ((i : Fin n) → Fin (d i)) ℂ))
    (hG : (compGraph d G).Connected) :
    (Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup ((i : Fin n) → Fin (d i)) ℂ : Set (Matrix _ _ ℂ)) ⊆
      denseGen G := by sorry

end BaconThesis.Universality
